{"title":"Subjectwise Test-11 Part Syllabus GATE 2026 EE Analog Electronics","fileName":"032_Subjectwise Test-11 Part Syllabus GATE 2026 EE Analog Electronics.html","branch":"ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING","series":"GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series Electrical Engineering","provider":"MADE EASY","duration":5400,"questions":[{"qnum":1,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>In the voltage reference circuit shown in the figure, the op-amp is ideal and the transistors <em>Q</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>Q</em><sub>2</sub>, ...... , <em>Q</em><sub>65</sub> are identical in all respects and have infinitely large value of common-emitter current gain (β). The collector current <img src=\"/media/347e69a6c7c1ed5054045f42caa7cb618d907336.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> of the transistors is related to their base-emitter voltage (<em>V<sub>BE</sub></em>) by the relation <img src=\"/media/9dd929c5f7f2122a83bb09414c6249a391275f43.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> is the saturation current. Assume that the voltage <em>V<sub>p</sub></em> shown in the figure is 0.7 V an the thermal voltage <em>V<sub>T</sub></em> = 26 mV.</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/47256bd5ba3a951d5ba1a9be1c1a5a376e0cb212.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div>The output voltage <em>V</em><sub>out</sub> (in volts) is _______ .</div></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"1.24","high":"1.24"},"solution":"<p>1.24 (1.22 - 1.26)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/7bd55572cd451f836dd5e194e002ced9363b44c6.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/5eddff24a580124c6ccb01bf3e8daae2fd77d9ca.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":2,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Two identical transistor shown in below figure have β = 200. Then output voltage <em>V</em><sub>0</sub> is</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/b6b338d93f7732aa50cb2dafe0f17301c1aa31e6.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 15.1 V</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 8.6 V</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 8 V</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 3.2 V</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/c0154c832d8d6241cc3c29dfaf53eb268b5ebb2c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":3,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>In the circuit shown below, take β = 150, <em>V<sub>BE</sub></em> = 0.7, <em>V<sub>T</sub></em> = 26 mV and <em>V<sub>A</sub></em> = ∞.<br />\nThe small signal voltage gain <em>V</em><sub>0</sub>/<em>V<sub>s</sub></em> = ________.<br />\n(Answer upto two decimal places)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/4cb6d4af4a746a9d4b8cc72529aac452fe79ae7b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"-7.43","high":"-7.43"},"solution":"<p>-7.43 (-8.00 - -7.00)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/2b3ab47481f08450433e54c101c3bf18e09a4c76.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":4,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>The output voltage of op-amp circuit is</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/85abc43743a654af1f930445f8c288353af19592.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 2.5 V</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 0 V</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 4.5 V</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 1.5 V</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/fa1e82c88779307f57fbd2ec78e7aa6c9183a804.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":5,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>A circuit consisting of capacitors, DC voltage 300 Volt and an amplifier having a voltage gain <em>A</em> = –10, is shown in the figure. The effective capacitance across nodes <em>A</em> and <em>B</em> (in μF) is _________. (Answer upto two decimal places)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/e766fa4372f0825da62d42552b2cfc14e74dc943.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"49.71","high":"49.71"},"solution":"<p>49.71 (49.00 - 50.00)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/536d427104b27a1bdfac2601ce8a1ea57edd5d09.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":6,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>In the circuit shown below, range of <em>R<sub>L</sub></em> that will result in <em>V<sub>RL</sub></em> being maintained at 20 V is</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/6fba0ba8e408f73be7aee72305134a15b99b5a56.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 5 kΩ ≤ <em>R<sub>L</sub></em> ≤ 25 kΩ</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 0.5 kΩ ≤ <em>R<sub>L</sub></em> ≤ 2.5 kΩ</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 0.5 kΩ ≤ <em>R<sub>L</sub></em> ≤ 5 kΩ</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 5 kΩ ≤ <em>R<sub>L</sub></em> ≤ 250 kΩ</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/8379fc2863d5a0fff7cb5b991afab1c98e5792b2.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/7f5735e8fb0ae69ee925dd151a438b2a94b24589.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":7,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,C","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Consider the circuit of transistor shunt regulator given below. The output of regulation is 24 V, when input of regular varices in range of 30 V to 40 V. The relevant parameters for the zener diode and transistor are <em>V<sub>Z</sub></em> = 14.1 V, <em>V<sub>BE</sub></em> = 0.7, β = 199. If the current through <em>R<sub>B</sub></em> is negligible, then which of the belo